One common way the Java API is misused is through the improper handling of user input when interacting with the operating system, such as when using `Runtime.getRuntime().exec()`. If input is not sanitized, it can lead to Command Injection.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class CommandExecutor {
    // This method is vulnerable to Command Injection because it 
    // concatenates raw user input directly into a shell command.
    public void executeSystemCommand(String fileName) throws IOException {
        String command = "ls -l " + fileName; 

        // If a user passes "; rm -rf /", the system executes the delete command.
        Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);

        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

***

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class CommandExecutor {
    // This method is secure because it uses a ProcessBuilder with a 
    // list of arguments, preventing the shell from interpreting 
    // special characters like ';' or '&' as new commands.
    public void executeSystemCommand(String fileName) throws IOException {
        // Treat the input strictly as a single argument for the 'ls' command
        List<String> command = Arrays.asList("ls", "-l", fileName);

        ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder(command);

        // ProcessBuilder handles argument separation, so "file.txt; rm -rf /" 
        // will simply result in an error stating that the literal file 
        // "; rm -rf /" does not exist.
        Process process = pb.start();

        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

